{
 "format": "jekray",
 "version": 1,
 "name": "Levitated nanorod trap (Hu et al. 2023)",
 "notes": "Optics of the standing-wave optical trap in Hu, Kingsley-Smith, Nikkhou, Sabin, Rodriguez-Fortuno, Xu & Millen, 'Structured transverse orbital angular momentum probed by a levitated optomechanical sensor', Nat. Commun. 14, 2638 (2023), Fig. 3a and Methods.\n\nBench plane: the paper's y-z plane (beam axis z runs left to right here, y is up the bench), so the beams' linear polarisation along y is p (in the plane: the fibre laser's polAngle 90, counted from s) and the offset delta_y that creates the transverse OAM is made as in the paper, by moving Launch B and Collimator B together along y. The two beams then still cross at the focus, at a small angle.\n\nReady to play: the view opens on the focus, and Launch B and Collimator B are swept together in y from -30 to +30 um (the same range, rate and phase, so the out-coupler moves as one, as in the paper). Press Play all: the field is worked out for 48 steps of the sweep first, the close-up included, then it plays as a movie. What to look at: at the focus (below about 50 um across) to see the standing wave, lambda/2 = 775 nm apart, with a waist of about 1 um as in the paper. Move Launch B and Collimator B together by a few tens of um in y to see the pattern change.\n\nFrom the paper: 1550 nm fibre laser, polarisation controller, amplifier, 50:50 fibre split into two arms; two fibre out-couplers; two NA 0.6 lenses (Thorlabs 355660-C, EFL 2.97 mm, clear aperture 3.60 mm) with coincident foci; ~0.6 W; 90:10 pick-off to the detectors on one side.\n\nAssumptions:\n- The amplifier is folded into the fibre laser's power (1200 mW after it, so ~0.6 W a beam); JEKray2D has no fibre amplifier.\n- 355660-C modelled as ideal lenses (mask with a perfect-lens delay, f = 2.97 mm, 3.60 mm aperture); its real asphere prescription is not given.\n- Out-couplers modelled as SMF-28-like fibre tips (core 8.2 um, NA 0.14) with ideal f = 15.4 mm collimators, chosen so the collimated beam (~2.9 mm diameter) fills the objective and focuses to w0 ~ 1 um as in the paper. The real out-couplers are not specified.\n- The 90:10 pick-off is drawn as a non-polarising cube reflecting 10%; the paper shows a plate. The detectors (quadrant photodiode, balanced homodyne, polarisation-resolved detection) are represented by one InGaAs photodiode.\n- The vacuum chamber windows and the nanorod are not modelled: JEKray2D models the light, not the particle or the forces on it.\n\nTo check: the collimator focal length; 355660-C mounting and working distance.",
